WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:01.220
(playful music)

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:02.910
- [Narrator] This is Northwest Arkansas,

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.940
the birthplace of Walmart and
the gateway to the Ozarks,

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.119
which you've probably heard of
because of that Netflix show.

00:00:09.119 --> 00:00:10.183
- You owe me to job?

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:12.480
- What?

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.400
- [Narrator] But for the most part,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.830
this little corner of the
country is off the radar

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:18.110
for today's legions of remote workers

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:18.943
who are itching to bounce

00:00:18.943 --> 00:00:21.340
from their high-priced,
high-density confines

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.703
in cities like San Francisco and New York.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.024
But, here they all are.

00:00:27.024 --> 00:00:28.080
- What?

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:28.950
- Are you serious?

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.520
(woman cheering)

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.870
- Duh, fantastic.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:32.780
- [Narrator] Getting a Zoom call

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:33.830
saying that they've been selected.

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:36.040
- I feel like I won the lottery.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.020
- [Narrator] To help put
Northwest Arkansas on the map.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.690
- How soon do you think
you could relocate here?

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:43.090
- Wow, okay.

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:45.540
- [Narrator] Among the many
incentivized relocation programs

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.090
attracting remote workers
since the pandemic,

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:51.380
this one in Northwest
Arkansas offers $10,000

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.680
to those who move to the area
and stay for at least a year.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.840
We're talking about
cities like Bentonville,

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.000
Rogers, and Fayetteville.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.390
- I did see that Fayetteville
was also top places

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.080
for singles to meet people.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.870
- [Narrator] They'll even
throw in a mountain bike.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.260
- Geez, $10,000 and a bike.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.210
- [Narrator] After receiving
more than 24,000 applications,

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.440
the program called their first 25 winners,

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:16.760
just a few weeks ago.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.590
- I wasn't expecting you to
tell me, so now I gotta--

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.960
- Oh goodness, I'm
definitely gonna call my mom.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.780
(Dazz laughs)

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.300
- [Narrator] It's
probably not a huge shock

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:26.790
that hundreds of applicants came

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.430
from the pricey tech hub of the Bay Area

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.720
and that includes the Portillo family,

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.100
Josue, his wife Athena, who both work

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.650
for tech companies and their three kids.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.050
- Thank God.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.523
I would looking for a sign.

00:01:41.918 --> 00:01:45.370
I was surprised and just full of joy.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:46.880
Me and my wife have been talking

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.830
about trying to get a home,
a new home for our family.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:55.460
- Our living situation in San
Jose is very, very compact.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:56.630
It's a two bedroom,

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.500
so all of us are in that little condo.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:00.680
- [Narrator] Before the pandemic,

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.500
Josue commuted four
hours a day from San Jose

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.220
for his job in San Francisco,

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.420
leaving just a few precious
hours left to spend

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.570
with his kids at night in
their 800 square foot home,

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.480
but even though he was
able to work from home

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.940
this past year, the Portillos still dreamt

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.320
of owning a big house, the kind they still

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.830
can't afford in the Bay Area.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.020
But now, with their $10,000 grant

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.910
and the blessing of Josue's employer,

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.510
the Portillos, which
includes Josue's parents,

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.120
say they'll be heading
to the Ozark Mountains

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.140
in a few months, where an average home

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:35.140
costs less than $270,000,
70% cheaper than San Jose.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.480
- When we went to go visit Arkansas,

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.203
it felt like a different country.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.100
It just felt like a secret
place that we found, like a gem.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.950
- [Narrator] The Portillos
are among the growing numbers

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.790
of remote tech workers
fleeing the broader Bay Area

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:51.630
and especially San Francisco.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.610
In fact, nearly 40% more people moved out

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:57.030
of San Francisco during
the pandemic months

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.820
compared to the same
period a year earlier,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.650
the largest increase of any
major city in the country,

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:05.630
according to data from
moving company, Updater.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.460
Most moved within California,

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.090
but these states were
also top destinations.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:12.730
While cities like Austin,
Denver, and Seattle

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:16.440
are enticing Bay Area tech
employees more than ever before.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.580
This comes as some of the
biggest tech companies

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:20.820
like Twitter, Facebook, and Salesforce,

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.890
San Francisco's largest private employer,

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.980
have all announced more
permanent remote work options.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.820
- I just never had given
a thought to Arkansas.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.460
- [Narrator] But if
smaller, less known places

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:32.250
like Northwest Arkansas wanna compete

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.400
for these remote workers,
they have to sweeten the deal,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.080
with cash and tax incentives
in the tens of thousands.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:40.330
- This is gonna be amazing.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.060
- [Narrator] Like Tulsa, Oklahoma did.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.740
Though it's $10,000
grants launched in 2018,

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:46.480
the program saw a dramatic rise

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.360
in applicants since the pandemic.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.720
It now has more than 500 participants

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.620
living and working in Tulsa.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:56.440
A newer program called Choose
Topeka pays up to $15,000

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.610
and has brought in 30
workers in just over a year.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.680
- Right out of the gate, it exploded.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.460
So, I mean, we had over 3,500 applications

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.550
within the first 30 days, globally.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:08.560
- [Narrator] Bob Ross
runs the Topeka program.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.260
- It really exposed a
tremendous pent up demand

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:15.140
for people looking for
something new and a fresh start

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.603
and they saw Topeka as
a possibility for that.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.210
- [Narrator] Having won a
$10,000 grant from Topeka,

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.380
Tyler Jaggers moved out of the Bay Area

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:27.320
and bought a $47,000 house in
Topeka that he's fixing up.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.200
- I was already thinking
about moving, pre-COVID,

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.253
and then once COVID kicked in,

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.660
coupled with things such as, myself,

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:39.270
I was living next to a
fire evacuation zone.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.110
- [Narrator] As an indie
video game developer,

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:44.020
who often dresses up for all
the networking events in town,

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.430
Tyler hopes it will be
easier to grow his company

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:47.870
with the lower cost of living.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:49.410
- It's a nice little sleepy town.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:50.680
It's not like bustling.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:51.970
There's not a ton of traffic.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:53.860
If you need to park, there's a spot.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.050
You got a big truck, there's a spot.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:59.140
Got a small car, enjoy it.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:00.623
So, it was just great fun.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.520
- [Narrator] Ross sees
remote workers, like Tyler,

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.333
as an investment, one that will pay off.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:08.940
- What we found is that $10,000

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.190
in remote incentives
translates into $50,000

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.600
of economic impact in just one year.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.010
And then, you add in
just the soft benefits

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:20.670
of the intellectual and cultural impact

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:21.610
that there'll be making.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.490
It's $10,000, very well-spent.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.580
- [Narrator] But skeptics
of these relocation programs

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:30.340
say the pandemic won't
last forever and then what?

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.040
San Francisco supervisor, Matt Haney,

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.330
believes the Bay Area
will always be the home

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.490
of innovators and entrepreneurs.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:40.150
- The tech industry has had
such a strong ecosystem here

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.140
that for people who wanna network,

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:46.140
who are starting businesses
and wanna recruit folks,

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.510
engineers, a lot of that
is concentrated here

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:50.570
in the Bay Area.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:52.560
- [Narrator] And even if
these relocated tech workers

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.110
don't return, that doesn't mean

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:56.740
a new wave of talent
won't take their place.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:59.000
- It may not be the same companies,

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.060
it may not be the same
people before the pandemic.

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.720
Only the biggest, richest companies

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.397
could even secure office space here

00:06:07.397 --> 00:06:10.200
and that may change,
which I think could create

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:14.602
a renaissance for startups
who wanna be in San Francisco.

00:06:14.602 --> 00:06:15.435
- [Girl] Did you saw that?

00:06:15.435 --> 00:06:17.010
Mommy, look at that big bubble.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.020
- [Narrator] The Portillos say

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.401
they don't expect to
return to the Bay Area.

00:06:20.401 --> 00:06:23.300
- I think the only way we would come back

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.640
is the cost of living goes down,

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.820
but I don't know if that's gonna happen.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:30.340
- [Narrator] But the
family says a fresh start

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:31.950
couldn't have come at a better time.

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:34.703
Several weeks ago, the entire
family contracted COVID.

00:06:34.703 --> 00:06:36.900
Josue's father was on a ventilator

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.500
and they weren't sure
he was going to make it.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.950
- When he finally came
home, it was a miracle.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:42.650
The doctors say it was a miracle

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:45.510
and then, a few days
later we got the grant.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.897
It just felt like that saying,

00:06:47.897 --> 00:06:50.029
"It's always darkest before the dawn."

00:06:50.029 --> 00:06:52.310
(Josue's father singing in Spanish)

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:55.020
- Josue's father is expected
to make a full recovery

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:56.640
and now, with some extra cash

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.880
and the promise of finding
affordable property,

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:01.890
Josue can live close to his
parents and look after them.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:06.890
Some sort of acre lot, maybe even placing

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:11.940
her house and my house next
to it, having a little ranch.

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:12.950
(laughs)

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:13.783
Yeah.

00:07:13.783 --> 00:07:16.356
(gentle music)

